sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS R2-R4, flow-tighten rules 1, 9, 16, 18, 22, single-swamp-node + light-boxes-use-swamp-ssh (vpn Node 1 has no Swamp worker). docs/reference/wireguard-endpoints.md.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: node:wireguard-hub:converge
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as vpn Node 1<br/>WireGuard hub
  CLI->>GW: wireguard-hub:converge 1<br/>orbit node:wireguard-hub:converge
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit node:wireguard-hub:converge 1`
  %%> - syntax: `<node> [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the Node with the `vpn` role, by ID or name
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - publishes the current peer list on the WireGuard hub; it changes no peer key or address
  %%> Also used by:
  %%> - `node:network:converge`, `node:wireguard-key` and Node removal run the same hub steps in their own run
  Note over GW: Validate, build the peers<br/>task #5171 · hub lock
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 1
  %%> - Node 1 holds the active `vpn` role and has stored SSH host keys
  %%> - no other hub change runs; the Gateway holds one lock for the hub and waits briefly for a busy one
  %%> Builds:
  %%> - the complete peer list: public key and allowed address of every active Node
  %%> Result:
  %%> - task #5171 recorded with the full intent; no private key in it
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-wireguard-hub
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - the peer list: name, public key and WireGuard address of every other active Node
  %%> - Node 1's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role
  %%> Not included:
  %%> - the hub's private key; it stays on Node 1
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5171
  %%> What you get:
  %%> - the task number, at once
  %%> Next:
  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`
  SW->>N: Read the live peers<br/>@swamp/ssh · wg show orbit peers
  %%> How:
  %%> - over SSH from 209 with the official `@swamp/ssh` behavior, as root; Node 1 has no Swamp worker
  %%> Why a read first:
  %%> - it picks the stale peers to remove; one `wg` argument list cannot both list and remove
  SW->>N: Set the peers<br/>wg set orbit peer … allowed-ips
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one `wg set` command adds or updates every admitted peer in the running hub
  %%> - the hub's own key and interface stay; unchanged peers keep their sessions
  opt a live peer is no longer admitted
    SW->>N: Remove stale peers<br/>wg set orbit peer K remove …
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - one `wg set` command removes every live peer that is no longer admitted
  end
  SW->>N: Save the config<br/>wg-quick save orbit
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - writes the running hub, with its own key, back to `/etc/wireguard/orbit.conf`, so a reboot keeps it
  SW->>N: Check the hub service<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/systemd check
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - checks `wg-quick@orbit` is running and enabled
  SW->>N: Read the peers again<br/>wg show orbit peers
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - reads the live peer list for the end check
  Note over SW: Confirm the hub<br/>native assert
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - fails the run unless the service check is compliant and the live peers are exactly the admitted ones
  %%> No rollback:
  %%> - a failed step leaves the hub as it is; a rerun sets the same list again
  alt the hub took the peer list
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Record the result<br/>hub published · task succeeded
    %%> Result:
    %%> - task #5171 succeeds with `projected: true`
  else a step or the check failed
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
    %%> The Gateway reads:
    %%> - which step failed and its exit code
    Note over GW: Mark the task failed<br/>rerun after a fix
    %%> Result:
    %%> - task #5171 fails; peers set before the failure stay
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run the same command again after fixing the cause; it is a new task
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5171
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended
    GW-->>CLI: status + result
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended
  end
